I verified the plan's claims against the live codebase and issue bodies. Now preparing the adversarial review.

Verdict: MINOR

Findings:
- [MINOR] Legacy-retirement rule remains conceptual, not pattern-level — `test_tier1_contract_defines_legacy_retirement_rule` (plan line 220) only asserts that "allowed and banned rule text" is present. The contract rule as quoted in pseudocode (lines 168-170) names "legacy product-doc references" as an abstract category but does not enumerate concrete patterns (filenames, path fragments, reference glyphs, or regex-able signatures). A child-issue implementer at `#2461-#2464` still has to reinvent what counts as "legacy product-doc reference" to enforce hygiene, so the rule passes the test at the prose level while failing the "concrete and implementable" bar the adversarial review demanded. Recommend adding an explicit enumerated list (e.g., banned glob/name patterns) and a test that asserts at least one concrete pattern is named.
- [MINOR] Workspace-hub-scoped mount path encoded into a tier-1-wide contract — `docs/standards/DATA_PLACEMENT.md:39-43` defines `/mnt/ace/` as a workspace-hub mount (sshfs automounts to `ace-linux-2`). The plan's Deliverable (line 150), AC (line 234), and test `test_tier1_contract_requires_repo_vs_ace_data_rule` (line 218) all quote `/mnt/ace/data` verbatim as the boundary. `digitalmodel`, `assetutilities`, and `aceengineer-website` are separate git repos; the contract needs to state whether (a) the literal `/mnt/ace/data/` mount is the universal tier-1 rule, or (b) each repo carries its own bulk-artifact mount and the rule is "repo-vs-bulk-artifact-store." Without that clarification, the contract is workspace-hub-shaped while being advertised as tier-1-wide.
- [MINOR] Checklist pseudocode promises coverage that no test enforces — pseudocode line 178 states the checklist must "record whether repo-vs-/mnt/ace/data placement guidance is covered" per repo, but the TDD list (lines 223-224) only asserts the checklist contains the four repo names and links `#2461-#2464`. No test asserts the checklist records per-repo data-placement coverage, per-repo operator-map status, or per-repo registry status — all three of which the issue body enumerates as routing surfaces. The checklist is therefore testable only at the coarsest level; an implementer could ship a checklist that lists repos and child issues without actually tracking surface-by-surface status.
- [MINOR] Every "requirement" test is a keyword-presence test, not a semantic test — tests on lines 212-217 (`test_tier1_contract_requires_agents_md`, `test_tier1_contract_requires_operator_map`, `test_tier1_contract_requires_machine_readable_registry`, `test_tier1_contract_requires_code_tests_docs_routing_table`, `test_tier1_contract_requires_source_hygiene_rules`) verify "requirement present" against contract text. The Claude review's criticism that routing-table semantics must be *independently testable* is met only at the textual-mention level — e.g., a contract that says "code/tests/docs routing table must exist" passes `test_tier1_contract_requires_code_tests_docs_routing_table` without actually specifying what a routing table *contains*. The test list does not require the contract to name the three table dimensions (code, tests, docs) independently or to name what a "canonical machine-readable registry" must include (path, schema, or filename constraint). This is the exact shallow-validation failure that Codex flagged.
- [MINOR] "code-placement" scope expanded without explicit scope-lock — issue #2460's live scope (verified via `gh issue view 2460`) defines code-placement as the answer to "where should code go?" via "code/tests/docs routing table requirements." The plan's deliverable additionally adopts the repo-vs-`/mnt/ace/data` boundary (the data-placement rule from `DATA_PLACEMENT.md`) in response to Codex's MAJOR finding. That broadening is defensible, but the plan does not state "this issue extends the #2460 scope to cover data placement adjacent to code-placement, justified by adversarial review." Without that explicit scope-lock note, a future reviewer reading only the issue body and the plan would see an apparent mismatch.
- [MINOR] Risk section unchanged from prior draft and does not acknowledge the Codex MAJOR on evidence dependency — the Revisions Required list (line 258-263) says the fix is to "treat [the scorecard] as local attestation rather than canonical repo state," and the Evidence-status note on lines 58-59 executes that — but the Risks section (line 267-273) does not list "contract-building depends on a local-only scorecard that is not yet in `main`" as a live risk. This is recoverable without a redraft but weakens the adversarial-review traceability.

Checks performed:
- Confirmed `docs/standards/CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md`, `FILE_STRUCTURE_TAXONOMY.md`, and `DATA_PLACEMENT.md` all exist locally and match the plan's claims (line 22-26, 62-64).
- Confirmed the three new artifacts do not yet exist locally: `docs/standards/TIER1_INDEXING_AND_CODE_PLACEMENT_CONTRACT.md`, `docs/standards/TIER1_INDEXING_CHECKLIST.md`, and `tests/docs/test_tier1_indexing_contract.py` — the plan's MISSING claims (line 65-67) are accurate.
- Confirmed `docs/reports/2026-04-22-tier-1-indexing-scorecard.md` and `docs/reports/tier-1-indexing-freshness-latest.md` exist locally.
- Read live issue #2460 body via `gh issue view`; confirmed the plan's scope aligns with the seven scope bullets in the live issue.
- Read live issue #2465 body via `gh issue view`; confirmed it is the daily-freshness audit issue, validating the plan's `test_tier1_contract_marks_2465_as_daily_freshness_followthrough` assertion (line 222).
- Checked rerun-focus item 1 (code-placement scope preserved): deliverable, AC, tests, and pseudocode all name "code/tests/docs routing tables" as an independent surface — preserved.
- Checked rerun-focus item 2 (routing surfaces independently testable): every named surface in the issue scope has its own `test_tier1_contract_requires_*` row on lines 211-217 — structurally independent, though semantically shallow (see MINOR above).
- Checked rerun-focus item 3 (repo-vs-/mnt/ace/data): encoded in deliverable (line 150), AC (line 234), pseudocode (line 165, 185), and test (line 218) — explicitly encoded.
- Checked rerun-focus item 4 (legacy retirement concrete): allowed/banned pair defined at concept level (line 168-170); test checks both phrases present (line 220); falls short of pattern-level enforcement — see MINOR above.
- Checked rerun-focus item 5 (#2465 tied to daily freshness): AC (line 237), pseudocode (line 186), test (line 222) all assert the linkage — explicit.
- Checked rerun-focus item 6 (scorecard as local attestation): Evidence-status note (line 58-59) and Documents-consulted note (line 33) explicitly downgrade the scorecard to local attestation and forbid the contract from depending on it — handled.
- Checked rerun-focus item 7 (narrow scope): Files-to-Change table (lines 197-202) lists three new files plus two updates, all documentation/test — narrow.
- Cross-checked the three review artifact paths on line 7 (claude/codex/gemini) are referenced in the Artifact Map (lines 142-144); did not verify file contents.

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
The rerun hits every structural check (each surface has its own test, each rule has its own AC), but every test in this plan is a text-presence assertion. That pattern is common for documentation contracts and it is the gap Codex already flagged once: "independently testable" is stronger than "has its own test row" — it means the test can fail when the semantics are wrong, not only when the text is missing. For a T2 docs contract this level of rigor is defensible, but the plan would be noticeably more robust if at least the repo-vs-data and legacy-retirement tests asserted enumerated content rather than a phrase match.
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`
